in @prism
in @meta
in @actor
in @option
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/reality

# @epistemologic/reality/time — the substrate's own time discipline.
#
# Four carriers, three frames, one convention. Time is the second
# species of @epistemologic/reality (silicon being the first): a
# substrate-wide vocabulary for measurable, observer-relative,
# hardware-floor-bounded values.
#
#   duration   non-negative scalar of nanoseconds (PG §2.5: pick
#              one, name it). The base carrier. Built via settle
#              so construction IS the proof of non-negativity.
#   monotonic  shift(duration): CLOCK_MONOTONIC-equivalent frame.
#              Substrate-internal. gen_prism tick deltas; compute
#              budgets; kintsugi flow time.
#   wall       shift(duration): CLOCK_REALTIME-equivalent frame.
#              Observer-relative. The shard provides the frame.
#   instant    shift(duration): wall-clock point-in-time for
#              human-facing scheduling/logs (PG's timestamptz
#              analogue measured from the frame's epoch).
#
# Precision: nanoseconds. ~584 years of range from a u64. Matches
# BEAM's erlang:monotonic_time(:nanosecond) native unit and the
# CLOCK_MONOTONIC/CLOCK_REALTIME kernel resolution. PG uses
# microseconds; PG's lesson is "pick one and name it" not "use μs
# specifically." The existing @time.tick is spectral time (event
# count), not wall units; no clash. The existing wall_time = u64
# ms and @beam.duration u64 carriers converge here under one
# precision; downstream call sites that knew "ms" or "unspecified"
# get nanosecond discipline structurally.
#
# Leap-second policy: duration is leap-second-free seconds (matches
# Unix epoch, BEAM, PG extract(epoch)). The @calendar/utc grammar
# (future) carries the leap-second-aware path at its adapter
# boundary. Per insight doc §2.5 / §8.6.
#
# Substrate-pull placement (2026-06-06, Mara's reality family migration):
# moved from boot/std/time.mirror to shards/epistemologic/reality/time.mirror
# under the @epistemologic/reality family root. Time is a species of
# reality the substrate has been carrying since the early boot — the
# migration recognizes that, lifts it under its proper family parent,
# and adopts the current shard discipline (prism block instead of the
# implicit boot ordering).
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-time-as-substrate-and-postgres-heritage.md.
# Spec context: shards/mirror/shard.mirror (the consumer that types
# `tick: tick`), fragmentation/docs/specs/lens-transit.md (the
# hardware-floor analogue at the measurement altitude).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]].
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/reality/time.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/reality/time` and only that.

# the base duration carrier. non-negative by construction (settle
# witness): constructors that would produce a negative duration
# return imperfect. u64 nanoseconds.
type duration = settle({ nanos: u64 })

# the two frames PG distinguishes as CLOCK_MONOTONIC vs
# CLOCK_REALTIME. shift(T): annotation-only. Same bytes, different
# declared shape; the type checker refuses to add a wall to a
# monotonic without going through an explicit convert(_) at a shard
# boundary. Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-parametric-types-and-fp-heritage.md
# §"shift(T) = annotation form".
type monotonic = shift(duration)
type wall = shift(duration)

# wall-clock point-in-time. Same bytes as duration; the declared
# shape names "nanoseconds since the frame's epoch, observer-
# relative." Used for human-facing scheduling, log timestamps, the
# carrier any cross-shard compare resolves through. Per insight doc
# §8.1 (open question 1, resolved): instant is needed as a separate
# frame because cross-shard messaging surfaces points-in-time that
# a single shard's snapshot tick cannot name.
type instant = shift(duration)

# a point-in-time capture of spectral state. every tick produces an
# oid. every oid is in the store.
#
# Promoted to `monotonic`: spectral ticks are the substrate's own
# monotonic time — every snapshot is one tick further from the
# root. The frame discipline is inherited; every existing consumer
# of `tick` (snapshots, deltas, replays, the gen_prism's tick
# field) gets the type-level guarantee that ticks compose only
# with ticks. Per insight doc §8.4 (open question 4, resolved).
type tick = monotonic
type snapshot = {
  oid: ref,
  tick: tick,
  loss: loss,
}

# ordered sequence of snapshots — the full history
type timeline = {
  head: snapshot,
  snapshots: [snapshot],
}

# position in the timeline — where the observer stands
type cursor = {
  current: snapshot,
  timeline: timeline,
}

# structural diff between two snapshots. not textual. the delta is
# a set of oid mutations.
type mutation = insert(ref) | remove(ref) | replace(ref, ref)
type delta = {
  from: snapshot,
  to: snapshot,
  mutations: [mutation],
  holonomy: loss,
}

# re-execution of the pipeline from a past state
type replay = {
  origin: snapshot,
  result: snapshot,
  delta: delta,
}

# a branch from a past state — new timeline, shared ancestry
type fork = {
  origin: snapshot,
  branch: timeline,
}

# === time actions ===
#
# Visit a past state (read-only observation). The present is not
# lost. The cursor moves. The state does not.
enter(s: snapshot) -> imperfect { \ }

# Bring a specific ref from the past to the present. The ref is
# content-addressed. If it exists, it restores. If the ref conflicts
# with present state, the result is partial.
restore(s: snapshot, r: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# Navigate the timeline — scroll through snapshots. Returns the
# cursor at the requested position.
browse(t: timeline) -> cursor { \ }

# Structural diff between two points in time. Returns the set of
# oid mutations, not a textual diff.
compare(a: snapshot, b: snapshot) -> delta { \ }

# Re-run the pipeline from this state. Deterministic: same oids in,
# same oids out. The new holonomy measures drift from original
# execution.
replay(s: snapshot) -> replay { \ }

# Create a branch from a past state. The fork shares ancestry. The
# branch diverges. Like git branch, but for spectral state, not files.
fork(s: snapshot) -> fork { \ }

# Move the cursor forward or backward by n ticks.
step(c: cursor, n: i64) -> cursor { \ }

# Return to the present — release the time travel view.
present(c: cursor) -> snapshot { \ }

# Convert an instant from one shard's frame into another's.
# Cross-frame `compare` is explicit per Alex's 2026-05-25 substrate
# decision: no hidden coercions; the relativistic conversion cost
# stays visible. Holes resolved by Fate at runtime via the shard's
# `parent: option(oid)` chain walk (per docs/insights/2026-05-25-
# shard-as-observer-relative-lambda-zero.md). The result is imperfect
# because the chain may not connect (two shards with no common
# ancestor cannot share an instant frame).
#
# Within a single frame, instants and durations compose freely
# (add(monotonic, monotonic) is total). Across frames, the type
# system requires this convert call before any comparison; the
# verifier rejects compare(a, b) where the frames differ without
# a convert in scope. PG's lesson §2.1 (timestamptz over timestamp)
# at the substrate altitude.
convert(target_frame: oid, src: instant) -> imperfect { \ }

out @epistemologic/reality/time
out duration
out monotonic
out wall
out instant
out tick
out snapshot
out timeline
out cursor
out mutation
out delta
out replay
out fork
out enter
out restore
out browse
out compare
out replay
out step
out present
out convert
